reational  use laboratories  meanwhile in mississippi montana and  south  montana and south dakota things haven't  gone so smoothly  opponents to legalization in all three  states have filed lawsuits  challenging the voter approved laws on  procedural grounds  many states did not have the time or  resources to move forward with ballot  initiatives due to the pandemic  and this and state shutdowns however  some states are pursuing  legalization because of the pandemic  both connecticut and pennsylvania have  been pushing for legalization  for recreational youth cannabis to  create revenue and jobs in their states  one study conducted by the connecticut  center of economic analysis suggested  that connecticut  could create 10 to 17 000 jobs and 690  to 740 million dollars in state  tax revenue over five years of sales  cannabis legalization measures are  already off to a great start for 2021.  there are already at least eight states  this year that have bills pushing for  some sort  of cannabis legalization including both  connecticut and pennsylvania  other states that have begun 2021  initiatives include  maryland nebraska new mexico north  dakota virginia and wyoming  i want to dive a little bit deeper into  what each state's initiative looks like  connecticut has introduced bill fb888  this will regulate this will legalize  and regulate adult use cannabis  the bill is expected to move forward  with the with amendments  but there have been no formal proposed  amendments yet  maryland has introduced hb 32 this bill  will  legalize and regulate adult use cannabis  from what it looks like this bill is in  the judiciary committee  with no amendments formally proposed yet  nebraska received enough signatures in  2020  to put medical marijuana on the 2020  ballot however  nebraska's supreme court stripped the  medical marijuana constitutional  amendment  from the 2020 ballot due to the question  being  confusing and creating voter doubts and  that it violated the single 