ized recreational use  oklahoma legalized medical use only  and texas has legalized cbd or hemp  products only  as you can see alaska and illinois are  legal for recreational use  but neither require iso accreditation  but states like maine nevada and now  vermont all have legalized recreational  use cannabis  and do require testing labs to have iso  iec  1785 accreditation new jersey maryland  and pennsylvania all have medical  cannabis programs  but iso iec's 17025 accreditation is not  required in new jersey  but is required in both maryland and  pennsylvania like i mentioned new jersey  has  just passed legalization for  recreational use but there's still no  requirements for iso iec  1705 accreditation yet  oregon is pretty unique when it comes to  testing cannabis they have their own  state accreditation program called orlap  warlab stands for oregon environmental  laboratory accreditation program  so they do not require cannabis labs to  seek accreditation from an iloc or  creation body  this program is what environmental labs  in oregon are assessed to  there's a little bit of controversy  about this because they also make  cannabis labs meet tni requirements  for example pt requirements are quite  stringent compared to iso requirements  tni requires labs to do pt of their  whole scope with the all analytes  included twice a year  this is very costly for labs that cannot  receive federal funding  most states do require some sort of  testing and do have testing requirements  even if iso iec 1725 accreditation is  not a requirement  i want to point out that in 2020 both  arizona and oklahoma went from not  requiring iso  iec 72025 accreditation to requiring it  i want to expand on why testing is now  required many states including both  arizona and oklahoma  legalized medical cannabis and didn't  exactly know where to go from there  in oklahoma they legalized medical  cannabis in 2018  but did not have any specific  requirements in place for this  they later realized they needed to add  specific testing requirements  like heavy metals microbials mycotoxins  thc potency  and a few others along with requiring  iso iec  17025 accreditation  the iso iec 7225  17025 standard is also easier to follow  considering  many other industries use this standard  you used to be able to get a license  before accreditation now you must get  accredited to get a state license  there are pros to cons there are both  pros and cons to this  a pro for requiring accreditation before  testing  or receiving a license is that  laboratories are being assessed  and being deemed competent before  testing customer samples  some cons for requiring accreditation  before testing  or receiving a license is cost most labs  do not have revenue before  they start testing so this is a big  upfront cost  assessors are not viewing any customer  generated data such as cfas  since the laboratory is not actually  testing also the validations are not  done with  in-matrix samples which could affect  results  states tend to evolve testing  requirements when they notice public  health trends such as recalls and  illnesses  for example in 2019 states started  noticing that there were an increased  number of people having respiratory  complications  due to vaping after further  investigation they later found that  vitamin e acetate was being used to cut  vaping products  this test is now required testing in  both maryland and michigan  as for recalls this could happen if some  someone found mold or foreign matter  in a product that has already gone to  shelf  some states in some states products are  also required to be  tested after they have been packaged and  are already ready to purchase  this is due to contaminants such as  microbials growing on  the product due to poor packaging or  storing oklahoma is just one state  adopting the system  several other states have revised their  testing requirements  in the past couple of years to require  additional testing  cali